## The 5 Grade Scenarios

A men's lab grown diamond ring is priced primarily by four factors: cut, color, clarity, and carat weight. IGI certification verifies all four. The grade scenario you choose determines both what the stone looks like and what it costs.

Here are the 5 grade scenarios, each under $2,000 for the stone:

---

### Grade Scenario 1 — G-VS2 (Practical Entry Grade)

**Who it's for:** Men who want a certified lab diamond without overpaying for grades invisible to the naked eye.

**G color** sits in the near-colorless range. On white gold, faint warmth shows under a loupe — not to the naked eye. **VS2 clarity** means minor inclusions visible under 10× magnification but completely invisible face-up.

For a 6–8mm [men's diamond wedding band](/mens-diamond-wedding-band), G-VS2 is a practical grade. The face-up appearance is clean — no visible color, no visible inclusions.

**Estimated stone price:** 1.5ct G-VS2 lab: ~$900. [Browse certified G-VS2 lab stones](https://www.bluenile.com/lab-grown-diamonds?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Natural equivalent:** 1ct natural G-VS2 from GIA: [$3,230](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29090690?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Lab 1.5ct G-VS2 is 50% cheaper with more carat weight.

---

### Grade Scenario 2 — F-VS1 (Enhanced Color, Clean Clarity)

**Who it's for:** Men who want a step up from G without paying for top-colorless grades.

**F color** is the boundary between near-colorless and colorless. F and D look different side-by-side under a lab light — not on a hand. For most men, F color is the smart compromise.

**VS1 clarity** tightens the inclusions to even smaller, fewer. The stone looks cleaner under magnification than VS2. Step-cut styles like [men's asscher cut rings](/mens-asscher-cut-ring) demand it — open facets expose inclusions that round brilliants hide.

**Estimated stone price:** 1.5ct F-VS1 lab: ~$1,200. [Browse certified F-VS1 lab stones](https://www.bluenile.com/lab-grown-diamonds?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Natural equivalent:** 1.5ct natural F-VS1 would cost $7,000–$9,000 for the stone alone. Lab saves approximately 85%.

---

### Grade Scenario 3 — E-VVS2 (Premium Clarity Tier)

**Who it's for:** Men who want VVS clarity — what gemologists call practically flawless.

**E color** is fully colorless. No warmth. No comparison needed.

**VVS2 clarity** means inclusions require a loupe to locate — even a skilled gemologist has to search. Face-up: perfectly clean. Under magnification: nearly so.

For a [men's round diamond ring](/mens-round-diamond-ring), E-VVS2 is the entry point to what most buyers describe as "flawless-looking."

**Estimated stone price:** 1.5ct E-VVS2 lab: ~$1,600. [Browse certified E-VVS2 lab stones](https://www.bluenile.com/lab-grown-diamonds?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Natural equivalent:** 1.5ct natural E-VVS2 costs $8,000–$11,000. Lab saves approximately 80%.

---

### Grade Scenario 4 — E-VVS1 (Top Clarity at E Color)

**Who it's for:** Men who want top clarity without paying for D color. E-VVS1 gives maximum clarity with one grade step down in color. That step is imperceptible face-up.

**VVS1 clarity** is the highest practical clarity grade. VS2 and VVS2 show more under magnification — but the gap from VVS1 only matters on paper.

This is the highest value grade in the lab diamond market. The jump from E-VVS1 to D-VVS1 adds ~$20 to the stone price at the lab tier. The jump saves $1,000+ at the natural tier.

**Exact stone price:** [IGI 1.50ct E-VVS1 Ideal Cut Round Lab-Grown Diamond — $1,930](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28134308?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Natural equivalent:** 1.5ct natural E-VVS1 costs $9,000–$12,000 for the stone. Lab saves approximately 80%.

---

### Grade Scenario 5 — D-VVS1 (Highest Grade Under $2,000)

**Who it's for:** Men who want the best-graded stone available — certified in writing by IGI.

**D color** is the highest color grade — no warmth, no yellow, no comparison.

It is the reference grade for all others. A D-VVS1 lab stone represents the apex of what IGI certifies.

The fact that this grade is under $2,000 for 1.5ct is a lab diamond market reality. The same grade in a natural stone costs $10,000–$12,000. IGI certifies this lab stone is chemically and optically identical to a natural D-VVS1.

**Exact stone price:** [IGI 1.50ct D-VVS1 Ideal Cut Round Lab-Grown Diamond — $1,950](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29116588?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Natural equivalent:** 1.5ct natural D-VVS1 would cost $10,000–$12,000. Lab saves approximately 80%.

---

## The Savings Equation

The savings between lab and natural diamond pricing are real and consistent. This is not a marketing claim. It is a certified market comparison.

Here is the data:

| Stone | Cert | Carat | Grade | Price | Natural Equivalent | Savings |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| [Lab Round](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29090690?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | GIA | 1.0ct | G-VS2 natural | $3,230 | — | — |
| [Lab Round](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28134308?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | IGI | 1.5ct | E-VVS1 lab | $1,930 | $9,000–$12,000 | 80%+ |
| [Lab Round](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29116588?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | IGI | 1.5ct | D-VVS1 lab | $1,950 | $10,000–$12,000 | 80%+ |
| [Lab Round](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28629934?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | IGI | 2.0ct | D-VVS1 lab | $2,810 | $20,000+ | 85%+ |
| **What this means** | IGI = same standard as GIA | Bigger lab = cheaper than smaller natural | D-VVS1 lab < G-VS2 natural | Savings are real and large | Spend the saved budget on the setting |

**Verdict:** The 1.5ct D-VVS1 lab stone at $1,950 costs less than the 1ct natural G-VS2 stone at $3,230. You get 50% more carat, two better color grades, VVS1 instead of VS2 — for 40% less. See the [platinum vs. white gold comparison](/round-diamond-platinum-vs-white-gold) for how to allocate those savings.

