## CVD vs HPHT — Does the Growth Method Matter?

Lab diamonds grow by two methods: CVD (Chemical Vapor Deposition) or HPHT (High Pressure High Temperature). Both produce chemically identical diamonds. Both are graded by IGI on the same 4C scale.

| Factor | CVD Method | HPHT Method | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|
| Growth process | Carbon gas deposits atom-by-atom onto a seed crystal | 1.5 million psi + 1,300°C mimics Earth's mantle | Different physics — identical diamond crystal |
| Typical growth time | 4–8 weeks per stone | 1–4 weeks per stone | Faster HPHT ≠ lower quality |
| Inclusion pattern | Feather or cloud inclusions at VS2 and below | Metallic flux inclusions possible at lower grades | At VVS grade, neither type is visible face-up |
| IGI certification | Graded identically to HPHT | Graded identically to CVD | Same 4C scale; method is irrelevant to grade |
| Most common for | Commercial lab production; dominates retail inventory | Specialty production; color-treated stones | Most lab stones at major retailers are CVD |
| **What this means** | Method doesn't change the grade | Buy on the cert, not the process | D-VVS1 CVD = D-VVS1 HPHT — identical grade |

**Verdict:** Don't base your purchase on CVD vs HPHT. IGI applies identical grading standards to both methods. A D-VVS1 is a D-VVS1 regardless of how it was grown.

VVS clarity protects you in step-cut styles like [men's emerald cut rings](/mens-emerald-cut-ring) and [men's cushion cut rings](/mens-cushion-cut-ring). The growing method does not.

