## Lab Diamond Price History — Why {{month_year}} is the Right Time

Lab diamond prices have dropped 80–85% since 2019. This is a certified market reality, not a marketing claim. Production scaling drove it — large CVD facilities came online between 2021 and 2024.

| Year | 1ct D-VVS1 Lab (est.) | 1ct D-VVS1 Natural | Savings | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2019 | ~$8,500 | ~$12,000 | 29% | Early lab market; premium pricing |
| 2021 | ~$5,800 | ~$12,500 | 54% | Production scaling begins |
| 2022 | ~$4,200 | ~$12,800 | 67% | Major producers increase yield |
| 2023 | ~$2,800 | ~$13,000 | 78% | Price decline accelerates |
| 2024 | ~$1,600 | ~$13,500 | 88% | Consumer demand shifts to lab |
| {{year}} | ~$1,300 (1ct); $1,950 (1.5ct D-VVS1) | ~$14,000+ | 86–89% | Current market; widest savings window |
| **What this means** | Prices fell 85% in 7 years | Natural prices rose ~17% | Spread is at all-time high | A $8,500 grade now costs $1,300 | Grade-for-budget is at its historical peak |

The price decline has stabilized since mid-2024. Prices fell fastest between 2022 and 2024 as supply outpaced demand.

The current $1,300–$1,950 range for D-VVS1 lab stones represents the floor of the correction. For the full independent analysis of certified inventory, see the [Blue Nile Men's Rings Review](/blue-nile-mens-rings-review).

The practical implication: a grade that cost $8,500 in 2019 now costs $1,950 at 1.5ct. The savings reinvested into a better setting, platinum metal, or larger carat weight are real and substantial.

